---
scope: src/cli/init
status: misaligned
description: >
  Cold-start installer that accepts exactly one optional argument —
  `--brain <integration>`, defaulting to `obsidian` — and ships the
  methodology layer (pointer stub, methodology doc set, pipeline slash
  commands, pipeline agent definitions, skill templates, the `aide` MCP
  server entry, the `aide-tree` launcher, IDE preview wiring) plus the
  bundled `brain.aide` template selected by `--brain` (with YAML null
  at unwired slots), while never writing a `.mcp.json` brain entry,
  never scaffolding the four entry-point artifacts (coding-playbook,
  study-playbook, update-playbook, research index), never creating the
  brain root directory, never asking the user for a brain path, token,
  connection URI, or any other backend-specific value, never
  overwriting existing files (and never touching anything under
  `.aide/config/` on subsequent runs), and surfacing a prominent
  terminal warning naming the right follow-up surface per category
  (`/aide:brain config` for filling the unwired slot and seeding
  entry-point artifacts, re-running the CLI with `--ide` for IDE
  choice when the IDE category was deferred, `/aide:upgrade` for drift
  in shipped artifacts) for anything it had to skip or defer.
intent: >
  The CLI bootstrapper is the sole cold-start installer — the one
  `npx` invocation that turns a cold project into a methodology-ready
  project end-to-end, without any Claude Code session yet. It runs in
  a plain terminal before any agent exists, so it has no user to
  interview: the only safe write policy is create-if-missing,
  skip-if-present, with the user-owned `.aide/config/` directory
  carrying an even stronger guarantee — once the CLI scaffolds a file
  inside that directory, the bytes belong to the user the moment they
  land and the CLI never touches them again on any subsequent run.
  There are no confirmation prompts. The non-help flag surface is
  intentionally narrow and storage-agnostic. Running `npx` is itself
  the opt-in.

  The cold install ships the methodology layer plus the bundled
  `brain.aide` template plus the `aide` MCP server entry. The
  methodology layer is the methodology pointer stub, the methodology
  doc set under `.aide/docs/`, the full set of pipeline slash
  commands, the full set of pipeline agent definitions, the full set
  of skill templates, the `aide-tree` launcher script, and IDE
  preview wiring when applicable. The bundled `brain.aide` template
  is the file the integration named by `--brain <integration>`
  (defaulting to `obsidian`) emits when invoked through the shared
  install service — for the default integration that means
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()` produces a `brain.aide` whose
  `mcpServerConfig.args` carries YAML null at the unwired slot
  (`args[3]`), and the file lands at `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The
  `aide` MCP server entry is additively merged into `.mcp.json` under
  the key `aide`. Brain MCP wiring and entry-point artifact seeding
  are NOT part of the cold install: the CLI never writes a
  `.mcp.json` brain entry, never scaffolds the four required
  entry-point artifacts (coding-playbook, study-playbook,
  update-playbook, research index), never creates the brain root
  directory, and never asks the user for a brain path, token,
  connection URI, or any other backend-specific value at install
  time. Those steps are fully delegated to `/aide:brain config` on
  the first `/aide` run inside Claude Code, where the integration's
  own `config` body section prose owns asking the user for whatever
  the chosen backend needs, editing `brain.aide` in place to fill the
  null slot(s), invoking `cli/sync` to derive the `.mcp.json` brain
  entry, and seeding the four entry-point artifacts via the brain's
  own MCP write tools.

  The CLI is intentionally storage-agnostic at the code surface. It
  does not enumerate brain backends, it does not branch on which
  integration was selected, and it never reads or introspects the
  bytes of the bundled template it scaffolds. The single recognized
  brain-shaped flag is `--brain <integration>`, whose effect is
  template-bundle selection only — the integration name picks which
  bundled template helper produces the `brain.aide` bytes; today
  `obsidian` (the default) is the only valid value. Every other
  brain-shaped flag — `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`,
  `--brain-token`, `--brain-url`, `--brain-name`, or any flag whose
  value is a backend-specific datum — is forbidden in any shape. The
  CLI never asks the user for backend-specific values; those belong
  to the integration's prose, surfaced through `/aide:brain config`
  inside an agent session that can hold a conversation, not through
  a one-shot terminal invocation that cannot.

  For every package-owned file it writes, the CLI checks disk first —
  if the file exists, the CLI skips it and records the skip. The CLI
  never overwrites. Files inside `.aide/config/` carry the parent
  spec's directory-as-ownership-boundary guarantee: once
  `brain.aide` (or any future user-owned config file) lands under
  that path, the bytes belong to the user forever and the CLI never
  patches, migrates, or rewrites them on any subsequent run
  regardless of byte drift from the canonical default. New defaults
  reach existing hosts through `/aide:brain config` (or direct user
  edit) for files inside `.aide/config/`, and through `/aide:upgrade`
  for shipped artifacts outside that directory — never through the
  install path.

  After the install pass, the CLI emits a prominent, hard-to-miss
  terminal warning — a loud ASCII-bordered block, not a routine log
  line — listing every file it skipped and every category whose
  setup remains for the user to finish, with each entry naming the
  right follow-up surface: brain wiring (the CLI scaffolded the
  template with a null unwired slot but did not fill it, did not
  derive the `.mcp.json` brain entry, and did not seed the four
  entry-point artifacts) points at `/aide:brain config`; deferred IDE
  configuration points at re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>`;
  skipped shipped artifacts (already-present canonical files that
  may have drifted from the package's current canonical content)
  point at `/aide:upgrade` for guided reconciliation. When the CLI
  finishes with nothing skipped and no IDE choice deferred, the
  warning is omitted and a plain "already set up" or "install
  complete" line is printed instead.

  The CLI must be safe to run repeatedly, must never destroy existing
  MCP entries or other files, must abort without writing if `.mcp.json`
  is malformed, must never mutate any file under `.aide/config/` on
  any run after the first scaffold, and must always leave the project
  in a state where the user can finish the job for anything the CLI
  could not do itself by following the per-category follow-up surface
  named in the warning.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - "Running the CLI installs every methodology-layer canonical artifact on every cold start: the methodology pointer stub, the methodology doc set under `.aide/docs/`, every pipeline slash command, every pipeline agent definition, every skill template, the `aide` MCP server entry (additively merged into `.mcp.json`), the `aide-tree` launcher script at `.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs`, and IDE preview wiring when applicable. The CLI is the sole cold-start surface that scaffolds these methodology-layer artifacts; no slash command duplicates this responsibility."
    - "Running the CLI also scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` from the bundled template selected by `--brain <integration>` (defaulting to `obsidian`), with YAML null at unwired slots in `mcpServerConfig.args` (for the default integration that means a bare `-` at `args[3]`). The CLI obtains the bytes by invoking the same `aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })` install-service path that `/aide:brain config` uses, but applies only the brain.aide-scaffold InitStep — the MCP-entry-plan InitStep is also returned and is deliberately NOT applied here (sync owns that write). Once the file lands, it is the user's: the CLI never patches, migrates, or rewrites it on any subsequent run regardless of byte drift from the canonical default."
    - "The CLI accepts exactly one brain-shaped flag, `--brain <integration>`, whose effect is template-bundle selection only — choosing which bundled template the install service will scaffold. Today `obsidian` (the default when `--brain` is omitted) is the only valid value; future integrations land their own bundled templates and become valid `--brain` values without changing this contract. The CLI never accepts any other brain-shaped flag (`--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`, `--brain-token`, `--brain-url`, `--brain-name`, or any flag whose value is a backend-specific datum), never asks the user for backend-specific values through stdin, an `--interactive` mode, or any other surface, and never branches on or introspects the bytes of the bundled template it scaffolds."
    - "The CLI writes no brain wiring beyond the `brain.aide` template. It does not write a `.mcp.json` brain entry (sync owns `mcpServers[\"brain\"]`), does not seed any of the four required entry-point artifacts (coding-playbook, study-playbook, update-playbook, research index — those need a brain root path the CLI never has), does not create the brain root directory, and does not reach outside the host project root. Filling the null slot in `brain.aide`, deriving the brain MCP entry, and seeding the entry-point artifacts are all the responsibility of `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run."
    - "For every package-owned file it writes, the CLI checks disk first and never overwrites. If the target file exists, the CLI skips it and records the skip for the final warning, with the warning naming `/aide:upgrade` as the follow-up surface for reconciling drift in shipped artifacts. Any path under `.aide/config/` carries a stronger guarantee: once a file inside that directory lands, it is never touched again on any subsequent run regardless of byte drift from the canonical default — the directory path itself is the ownership signal, with no per-file allowlist. Skip-on-exists is the only safe branch for shipped artifacts; presence-once-then-hands-off is the only safe branch for `.aide/config/`. The CLI has no user to consent to either form of overwrite, and reconciliation against the package's current canonical content for shipped artifacts is `/aide:upgrade`'s job; reconciliation of `.aide/config/` content is the user's via `/aide:brain config` or direct edits plus `npx aidemd-mcp sync`."
    - "The `aide` MCP server entry is always additively merged into `.mcp.json` — if the file does not exist it is created with just the `aide` entry; if it exists with other server entries (`github`, custom servers, etc.) every existing entry is preserved and only `aide` is added. The key is `\"aide\"` (matching the dual-key check `\"aide\" in servers || \"aidemd-mcp\" in servers`)."
    - "If `.mcp.json` exists but contains malformed JSON, the CLI prints a clear error naming the file, exits non-zero, and writes nothing — it never clobbers a broken config."
    - "The recognized flag surface is `--help`, `--ide <choice>`, and `--brain <integration>`. Each non-help flag is a power-user shortcut to supply, up-front, a value that selects a methodology-layer artifact bundle: `--ide` selects which IDE preview wiring scaffolds; `--brain` selects which bundled `brain.aide` template scaffolds. Neither flag prompts the user, neither flag accepts a backend-specific datum (path, token, URI, vault root), and flags that gate behavior unrelated to artifact selection (verbosity, dry-run, force, framework selection) are out of scope for the cold-start CLI."
    - "When `--ide <choice>` is supplied, the CLI applies the IDE configuration for that choice; when absent, IDE is the only category that defers entirely (no scaffold lands at all) and the deferred list in the final warning names re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>` as the follow-up. The CLI never guesses, defaults, or scans the filesystem to fill this decision."
    - "After the install pass, the CLI prints a prominent, hard-to-miss terminal warning block — loud ASCII borders, uppercase heading, clearly separated from the per-file log lines — that names every file it skipped (because it already existed) and every category whose setup remains for the user to finish, and tells the user the right follow-up surface per category: brain wiring (the CLI scaffolded `brain.aide` with a null unwired slot but did not fill it, did not derive the brain MCP entry, and did not seed the four entry-point artifacts) → run `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run; deferred IDE (because `--ide` was not supplied) → re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>`; skipped shipped artifacts → run `/aide:upgrade` for guided reconciliation."
    - "When the install pass has nothing to warn about — every file the CLI would have written already existed and there is no IDE deferral — the warning block is omitted and a plain `Already set up.` or `Install complete.` line is printed instead, with no follow-up surface named because there is nothing left for the CLI to follow up on. (Brain wiring's follow-up surface is owned by `/aide:brain config` on first `/aide` run regardless of CLI state, so it is not a CLI-side condition that triggers the warning.)"
    - "Per-file terminal output is terse and scannable: one line per artifact with a `[created]` or `[exists]` status prefix. No colors, no spinners, no progress bars, no ASCII art in the per-file log — the loud ASCII is reserved for the final warning so it stands out."
    - "Running the CLI is idempotent. A second run on a project where the CLI already completed its install writes no files, reports every artifact (including the previously-scaffolded `brain.aide`) as `[exists]`, and either prints the `Already set up.` line (if no IDE deferral remains) or re-prints the same warning as the first run."
    - "The CLI completes its default happy path with no user interaction beyond running `npx` itself — no confirmation prompts, no `y/N` gates, no in-run input requests. Flags are accepted only when they were supplied on the invocation line, and even then they must not block on user input mid-run."
    - "Exit code is 0 when the install pass completes without error, regardless of whether files were skipped or IDE was deferred. Exit code is non-zero only for actual failures (malformed `.mcp.json`, unwritable filesystem, missing canonical content)."
    - "After the CLI completes, the per-category follow-up surfaces named in the warning are sufficient — and individually executable — to finish the setup for every skipped file and every incomplete category. Brain wiring is finished by running `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run inside Claude Code (the integration's own `config` prose owns asking the user for whatever the backend needs, editing the scaffolded `brain.aide` to fill the null slot(s), invoking `cli/sync` to derive the `.mcp.json` brain entry, and seeding the four required entry-point artifacts via the brain's own MCP write tools); IDE is finished by re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>`; skipped shipped artifacts are reconciled by running `/aide:upgrade`. No single surface owns the whole follow-up, and the user is never told to run a removed slash command."
  undesired:
    - "The CLI writes a `.mcp.json` brain entry from inside the cold-start install path. The brain MCP entry is written by `cli/sync` invoked from `/aide:brain config` after the user has filled the null unwired slot in `brain.aide`. Writing the brain entry from `cli/init` would land a `mcpServers[\"brain\"]` whose `args` carry YAML null at the unwired index — exactly the shape sync refuses at its boundary — and creates a path where `.mcp.json` carries a brain entry that does not derive from a fully-wired `brain.aide`. Even though the install service returns the MCP-entry-plan InitStep alongside the brain.aide-scaffold InitStep, `cli/init` deliberately applies only the latter."
    - "The CLI seeds any of the four required entry-point artifacts (coding-playbook, study-playbook, update-playbook, research index) on cold install. Entry-point seeding needs a brain root path the cold-start CLI never has and never asks for; it happens later through the integration's `config` body section prose during `/aide:brain config`, after the user has filled the null slot and sync has wired the brain MCP entry. Seeding from `cli/init` would either guess a brain root or read the just-emitted `brain.aide`'s args (introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args` is forbidden by the parent intent) — both regressions of the same class."
    - "The CLI creates the brain root directory (or otherwise touches the filesystem location the user's brain will eventually live at) on cold install. Brain root creation is the integration's responsibility, surfaced through `/aide:brain config` after the user has chosen and configured a backend. The cold-start CLI never reaches outside the host project root for brain-related filesystem state."
    - "The CLI asks the user for a brain path, API token, connection URI, vault root, or any other backend-specific value at install time — through stdin prompts, an `--interactive` mode, an environment-variable read with a confirm step, or any other surface. Backend-specific values belong to the integration's prose surfaced through `/aide:brain config` inside an agent session, not to the cold-start terminal invocation. (Writing a `.mcp.json` brain entry, seeding entry-point artifacts, creating the brain root directory, and asking for backend-specific values are the same regression class — the CLI doing brain wiring beyond scaffolding the bundled template.)"
    - "The CLI accepts any brain-shaped flag other than `--brain <integration>` — `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`, `--brain-token`, `--brain-url`, `--brain-name`, or any other flag whose value is a backend-specific datum the user would otherwise supply through `/aide:brain config`. The CLI's storage-agnostic invariant forbids the entire flag class with one carve-out: `--brain <integration>` whose effect is template-bundle selection only (today the only valid value is `obsidian`), with no backend-specific values exchanged at install time."
    - "The CLI branches on or introspects the bytes of the bundled `brain.aide` template it scaffolds — reading `mcpServerConfig.args` to extract a path, parsing a body section, hardcoding a slot index like `args[3]`, or otherwise letting code know which slot means what. Slot semantics live exclusively in the per-integration `config` body section prose (parent intent #25); the CLI's job ends at `aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })` returning bytes and `cli/init` writing them to disk."
    - "Any literal-string placeholder appears in the `brain.aide` bytes the CLI scaffolds — `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, `<VAULT_ROOT>`, `${BRAIN_PATH}`, `__UNWIRED__`, magic UUIDs, or any other in-band string the package recognizes as \"fill me in\". YAML null is the structural unwired-slot signal the bundled template emits and the only one this package ever ships; literal-string sentinels are retired entirely (parent intent #20) and reintroducing one under any name re-creates the byte-for-byte-agreement-passes-drift-check silent-failure bug that the YAML-null design fixes."
    - "The CLI overwrites an existing file because it assumed the on-disk copy was stale or out of date. The CLI has no user to consent to an overwrite — skip-on-exists is the only safe branch, and any reconciliation against the package's current canonical content must be deferred to `/aide:upgrade`. The same prohibition applies with extra force to anything under `.aide/config/`: once `brain.aide` (or any future user-owned config file) lands in that directory, even if the on-disk content has drifted from the canonical default, even if a newer bundled template would be more correct, even if the user re-runs init, the CLI must not patch, migrate, or rewrite the file."
    - "The CLI finishes with files skipped or categories incomplete but prints no warning, leaving the user to discover their install is incomplete only when they later trip over a missing agent, a missing command, or a missing MCP entry."
    - "The warning about skipped files and incomplete categories is quiet, inline with the routine `[created]/[exists]` log lines, or formatted in a way the user can miss when skimming. The warning must be unmissable — loud ASCII borders, clear separation, and explicit per-category follow-up instructions naming the right surface for each (`/aide:brain config` for filling the unwired slot and seeding entry-point artifacts, re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>` for IDE, run `/aide:upgrade` for skipped shipped artifacts)."
    - "The warning block is printed even when there is nothing to warn about. A clean install with no skips and no IDE deferral must print a plain completion line, not a loud box with an empty body."
    - "The CLI attempts IDE configuration (Zed, VS Code extension install, file-association edits) on a run where `--ide` was not supplied. IDE choice is an inherent decision; without the flag, the only correct path is to defer the category and tell the user to re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>`."
    - "Overwriting or removing existing MCP server entries that belong to other tools when merging the `aide` entry into an existing config. The merge must be purely additive."
    - "Silently succeeding while writing malformed JSON to `.mcp.json`, leaving the project in a state where no MCP servers load."
    - "Clobbering an existing `.mcp.json` that contains malformed JSON. The CLI must detect the parse failure, print a clear error naming the file, exit non-zero, and leave the file untouched."
    - "Writing the aide MCP entry under a key other than `\"aide\"` (for example `\"aidemd-mcp-server\"` or `\"aide-server\"`). The dual-key check `\"aide\" in servers || \"aidemd-mcp\" in servers` is the contract for the aide entry; any other key causes downstream tooling (including `/aide:upgrade`, `/aide:brain config`, and `npx aidemd-mcp sync`) to see no existing entry and offer to add a duplicate."
    - "Prompting the user interactively on the happy path — `y/N` gates, conversational questions, or any in-run input request. The CLI is opt-in by virtue of the user running `npx`; further confirmation adds friction to a non-interactive bootstrap. The interactive surface for brain wiring is `/aide:brain config` inside Claude Code, not the CLI."
    - "Adding flags that gate behavior unrelated to selecting a methodology-layer artifact bundle — verbosity toggles, dry-run, force-overwrite, framework selection, dotfile discovery, network opt-in, telemetry. The non-help flag surface is reserved exclusively for power-user shortcuts that select which bundle scaffolds (`--ide <choice>` for IDE preview wiring, `--brain <integration>` for the bundled `brain.aide` template). Anything else is scope creep that drags the cold-start CLI toward a general-purpose configuration tool it must not become."
    - "The CLI aborts the whole install on the first per-file failure. An unwritable agent file must not prevent the methodology stub, the slash commands, the MCP merge, the bundled `brain.aide`, and the launcher from being installed — failed writes go into the final warning alongside skipped files, and the CLI continues."
    - "The CLI reaches for the network. Every artifact it writes is checked into the package; an offline machine must get the same result as an online one."
    - "The terminal warning, the per-file log, the `--help` output, or any other CLI-emitted text directs the user to a removed or non-existent slash command (e.g., `/aide:init`, or `/aide` as a brain-wiring inline-recovery entry). Every follow-up surface named in CLI output must resolve to a currently-shipped surface: `/aide:brain config` for brain wiring, re-running the CLI itself with `--ide <choice>` for IDE configuration, or `/aide:upgrade` for reconciling skipped shipped artifacts. Naming a removed surface looks helpful but leaves the user chasing a command that no longer exists."
    - "The CLI writes `.mcp.json` (or mutates any MCP entry) through any path other than the shared `writeMcpEntry` helper. All `.mcp.json` mutations in the CLI must flow through the shared helper to guarantee uniform key-preservation semantics and the abort-on-malformed-JSON contract."
---

## Context

Every AIDE adoption runs into the same chicken-and-egg: the interactive bootstrap tool that would normally set up a project (`aide_init`) lives behind the MCP server, and the MCP server cannot be invoked until it has been registered in the project's MCP config. Before any agent session can help, something else must place those first files — and that something has to run in a plain terminal, before any Claude Code session, with no agent available to interview the user. The CLI bootstrapper is that something. It is the sole cold-start surface that turns an empty directory into a methodology-ready project end-to-end, the only step in the lifecycle that runs without an agent on the other end of the wire, and the only hop where the user is reachable solely through stdout and exit codes.

That terminal-only context drives the whole shape of the contract. There is no agent to hold a conversation with the user, no MCP tool surface to call, no `AskUserQuestion` channel to gather the kind of structured information a backend needs to be wired. The CLI cannot interview, cannot probe, cannot even safely guess: any datum that requires a backend-specific decision (an absolute brain path, an API token, a connection URI, a database name) has to come from somewhere with more bandwidth than a one-shot `npx` invocation. That somewhere is the first `/aide` run inside Claude Code, where the agent sits inside a session that can read tools, call `AskUserQuestion`, hold state across turns, and run a multi-step wiring flow. The cold-start CLI's job is everything that *can* be done without that bandwidth — the methodology layer — and the single follow-up surface its terminal warning routes to (`/aide:brain config`) is where everything that *needs* that bandwidth gets done.

The methodology layer is package-owned and identical for every host: the framework pointer stub the agent reads first, the methodology doc set at `.aide/docs/`, the full set of pipeline slash commands, the full set of pipeline agent definitions, the skill templates, the `aide` MCP server entry merged additively into `.mcp.json`, the `aide-tree` launcher script, and IDE preview wiring when `--ide` is supplied. Every one of these artifacts has the same shape across every host project the package will ever be installed into; none of them carry a backend-specific value or branch on which brain the user will eventually choose. They are exactly the artifacts the cold-start CLI can safely write without an agent in the loop.

The brain layer carries values that are inherently per-host (which backend, where its data lives, what credentials it needs), and the canonical home for those values — the user-owned `.aide/config/` directory established by the parent intent — carries a directory-as-ownership-boundary guarantee: any file landed inside that directory belongs to the user the moment it touches disk, and no install or upgrade tool ever patches, migrates, or rewrites it on a subsequent run regardless of byte drift from the canonical default. The cold-start CLI cannot supply the backend-specific values, but it can ship the bundled template's package-owned bytes with the unwired positions left as structural YAML null — a bare `-` in `mcpServerConfig.args` that the parser maps to JS `null` and that the package recognizes structurally as "the user has not yet supplied this value." That is the third path between "the CLI lands a file with backend-specific values it has no way to obtain" and "the CLI lands a file with literal-string placeholders the package would later have to recognize." The literal-placeholder design is retired explicitly by the parent's `outcomes.undesired` because it produced a silent-failure-at-launch bug when byte-for-byte agreement on the placeholder slipped past every drift check; YAML null replaces it as the structural signal and the only one the package ever ships. The CLI's job inside the brain layer is exactly one slice: scaffold the bundled template's bytes (with whichever positions YAML null marks as unwired) so the file lands and the user-owned ownership clock starts. Everything past that — filling the null slot(s), deriving the `.mcp.json` brain entry through `cli/sync`, seeding the four entry-point artifacts via the brain's own MCP write tools, creating the brain root directory if applicable — is `/aide:brain config`'s job, on the first `/aide` run inside Claude Code where the agent has the bandwidth to run a multi-step wiring flow.

The cold-start CLI's job ends one hop before the wiring proper. It ships the methodology layer, scaffolds the bundled `brain.aide` template (selected by `--brain <integration>`, defaulting to `obsidian`), writes the `aide` MCP server entry into `.mcp.json`, prints a per-file log, surfaces a prominent terminal warning naming the right follow-up surface per category, and exits. The user runs `npx`, then opens Claude Code and runs `/aide`, then `/aide:brain config` fills the null slot, runs sync, and seeds the entry-point artifacts. Three steps, each with one job, each owned by the surface that has the right bandwidth for it.

## Strategy

**The cold install ships the methodology layer plus the bundled `brain.aide` template with YAML null at unwired slots; every brain surface that needs a backend-specific value is fully delegated to `/aide:brain config`.** Cold install is the surface with no agent on the wire and no user-bandwidth channel beyond stdout — every artifact it writes has to be safe under that constraint. The methodology-layer artifacts (the framework pointer stub, the `.aide/docs/` doc set, every pipeline slash command, every pipeline agent definition, every skill template, the `aide` MCP server entry, the `aide-tree` launcher, IDE preview wiring) are package-owned, identical for every host, and carry no backend-specific values; they meet the bar. The bundled `brain.aide` template meets the same bar in a subtler way: every byte of it ships in the package, the template helper takes no per-host argument, and the unwired slot in `mcpServerConfig.args` is structural YAML null — not a value the CLI had to supply, not a literal-string sentinel the package would later have to recognize, just a bare `-` the parser maps to JS `null`. The CLI writes that template byte-for-byte and stops. The artifacts that genuinely require per-host values — the `.mcp.json` brain entry (sync's job, refused while args carry null), the brain root directory, the four entry-point artifacts that need a brain root path to land at — fail the no-bandwidth bar by construction; the CLI does none of them. The clean partition is: ship the methodology layer, ship the bundled brain template with structurally unwired slots, never write a `.mcp.json` brain entry, never seed any entry-point artifact, never create the brain root, never ask the user for any backend-specific value. The follow-up surface that has the bandwidth — `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run inside Claude Code, where `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` re-seeds the bundled template if absent and `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` returns the integration's wiring prose the agent executes verbatim — owns every step the CLI cannot. This partition is what the frontmatter's first two desired outcomes ask for and what the entire `cli/init writing brain wiring beyond scaffolding the bundled template` regression class in the parent's `outcomes.undesired` rules out.

**Exactly one brain-shaped flag is recognized — `--brain <integration>` — and its effect is template-bundle selection only; every other brain-shaped flag is forbidden.** The cold install needs to know one thing about the brain layer to do its job: which bundled template's bytes to write into `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The integration name carries that signal and nothing more — it picks which bundled template helper produces the bytes, and the helper takes no parameters. Today the only valid value is `obsidian` (the default when `--brain` is omitted), and the helper is `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()` which emits a `brain.aide` whose `mcpServerConfig.args` carries YAML null at `args[3]`. Future integrations land their own bundled template helpers and become valid `--brain` values without changing this contract. The flag exchanges no backend-specific datum at install time: it does not accept a path, a token, a connection URI, a database name, or any other value a particular backend's wiring would need. Every other brain-shaped flag is forbidden by the whole shape, not a list of names — `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`, `--brain-token`, `--brain-url`, `--brain-name`, and any flag whose value is a backend-specific datum are all out, regardless of how innocuous the surface looks. The reason is the same as the parent's storage-agnostic invariant: a flag like `--brain-path` would force the cold-start CLI to know the shape of one backend's wiring (an absolute path, for the bundled obsidian default), bias the CLI toward making that backend's slot feel canonical, and start asking the user for backend-specific values from a terminal surface that has no agent on the wire to confirm anything. `--brain <integration>` is the bounded escape hatch that sits one level above any backend: it picks which bundled template ships, never what the user fills into it. Everything that requires backend-specific values stays behind the agent-session bandwidth boundary at `/aide:brain config`.

**The brain template is obtained by calling the same install service `/aide:brain config` uses, but only the brain.aide-scaffold InitStep is applied; the MCP-entry-plan InitStep is deliberately skipped.** The bytes the CLI writes into `.aide/config/brain.aide` come from `aide_init({ category: "brain" })`, which routes through `provisionBrain` and returns exactly two InitStep records — the brain.aide-scaffold step (writes the bundled template selected by the integration name with YAML null at unwired slots) and the MCP-entry-plan step (would derive the `.mcp.json` `brain` entry from the just-written `brain.aide`). The CLI applies only the first. Skipping the second is load-bearing on two fronts: derived from a brain.aide whose `mcpServerConfig.args` carries null entries, the MCP-entry-plan step would land a `mcpServers["brain"]` whose args are null at the same indexes — exactly the shape `cli/sync` refuses at its boundary by exiting non-zero, and exactly the shape that previously masked the silent-failure-at-launch bug when literal-string sentinels propagated byte-for-byte through both files. Sharing the install service entry point with `/aide:brain config` (rather than reimplementing the scaffold inline) keeps the bundled template's contents flowing through one canonical writer regardless of which surface invoked it; that is what makes `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` self-heal a missing `brain.aide` later when `/aide:brain config` calls it unconditionally. The CLI's role is one slice of that pipeline — the brain.aide bytes only — and the deliberate non-application of the MCP-entry-plan step is what keeps the cold-install surface within the no-null-mcp-entry contract.

**The terminal warning is the single follow-up surface routing layer; it owns the hand-off from CLI to agent.** Even though the CLI scaffolds the bundled `brain.aide` template, the file lands with a structurally unwired slot — a YAML null at one or more positions in `mcpServerConfig.args` — and no `.mcp.json` brain entry, no entry-point artifacts, no brain root directory. Brain wiring is genuinely undone every cold install, and the user has to be told. The terminal warning is that telling. Its design has to break the visual rhythm of the per-file log enough that a user skimming `[created]`/`[exists]` lines cannot miss it: a loud ASCII-bordered block, an uppercase heading like `SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED`, clear separation from routine status lines, and a per-category follow-up entry naming the right surface inline. Brain wiring routes exclusively to `/aide:brain config` (which fills the null unwired slot, derives the brain MCP entry through `cli/sync`, and seeds the four entry-point artifacts via the brain's own MCP write tools); deferred IDE choice routes to re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>`; skipped shipped artifacts (already-present canonical files that may have drifted from the package's current canonical content) route to `/aide:upgrade`. Each follow-up surface owns one category, no overlap, no fallback to a removed slash command — the parent's "directs the user to a removed slash command" undesired outcome rules out naming `/aide` itself for brain wiring (the orchestrator does not own brain-wiring inline recovery; `/aide:brain config` does) or naming any retired surface like a hypothetical `/aide:init`. When the install pass has nothing to warn about — every artifact reported `[exists]` and no inherent-decision category remains unresolved — the warning block is omitted entirely and a plain `Already set up.` or `Install complete.` line prints instead, because an empty loud box trains users to ignore the warning surface and destroys its signal in the runs that genuinely need it. The brain-wiring follow-up is owned by `/aide:brain config` regardless of disk state, so it is not a CLI-side condition the warning ever needs to compute — the warning carries it on every run where any other category is also incomplete, but a fully-clean re-run with no IDE deferral suppresses the warning block entirely because the brain follow-up is permanent rather than a CLI-skip event.

**`.aide/config/` is the user-owned directory; the cold-start CLI scaffolds `brain.aide` exactly once on a fresh install and never touches anything inside the directory on any subsequent run.** The parent intent establishes that any file under `.aide/config/` belongs to the user the moment it lands and stays untouched on every subsequent install or upgrade run, regardless of byte drift from the canonical default. The cold-start CLI is the surface that starts that ownership clock for `brain.aide`: on a fresh install it creates the directory and writes the bundled template's bytes, then the file is the user's forever. On any subsequent run — whether the user re-runs `npx aidemd-mcp init` because they changed `--ide`, or because they want to verify the install, or for any other reason — the CLI checks for the file's presence first, reports `[exists]` if it is there, and never patches, migrates, or rewrites the bytes regardless of how far they have drifted from the canonical default. The boundary is the directory path itself, not a per-file allowlist: any future user-owned config file added under `.aide/config/` inherits the same once-then-hands-off semantics automatically. The CLI never introspects the bytes it scaffolded, never reads back the file it just wrote, and never branches on what is inside any `.aide/config/` file — the directory is owned by the user from the moment its first file lands, and reconciliation against newer canonical defaults is `/aide:brain config`'s job (for re-seeding when the file is missing) or a deliberate user edit (for in-place changes the user authors), never the install or upgrade path.

**Skip-on-exists is the only safe branch for shipped artifacts; reconciling drift is `/aide:upgrade`'s job.** Every package-owned methodology-layer artifact follows the same three-step shape: resolve the absolute host path, `fs.access` the path, and if present record a skip with a stable reason string. If absent, create the parent directory tree, read the canonical content from the package, write the file, and record a create. Overwriting an existing file because the on-disk copy *might* be stale is forbidden: the CLI has no user to consent to an overwrite, no UI to surface a diff, and no signal to distinguish a hand-customized doc from a stale canonical default. Drift in shipped artifacts is reconciled through `/aide:upgrade`, which runs inside an agent session that *can* surface a diff and ask for consent. The CLI's role is just to log the skip and route the user there through the warning. Any artifact whose canonical read fails (e.g., the package is partially unpacked) records a skip with the error message as its reason and does not crash the rest of the run.

**MCP merge is read-parse-merge-write with the dual-key existence check and abort-on-parse-failure.** The cold-start MCP write is the `aide` entry only — additively merged into `.mcp.json` under the key `"aide"`, with the dual-key existence check `"aide" in servers || "aidemd-mcp" in servers` so a re-run on a project that already has the entry reports `[exists]` instead of adding a duplicate. The merge is purely additive: every existing server entry (`github`, custom servers, a legacy `obsidian` key from a pre-rework install) is preserved byte-for-byte. If `.mcp.json` exists but contains malformed JSON, the CLI prints a clear error naming the file, exits non-zero, and writes nothing — clobbering a broken config would leave the user in a worse state than they started. The brain entry is *not* written by the CLI under any circumstance, even though `cli/init` itself scaffolds the `brain.aide` template; deriving the brain MCP entry would require reading `mcpServerConfig.args` from a file whose unwired-slot indexes carry YAML null, and writing the resulting null-bearing entry into `.mcp.json` would land exactly the shape sync refuses at its boundary. The brain entry instead lands later via `cli/sync` invoked from the integration's `config` prose during `/aide:brain config`, after the user has filled the null slot(s) and every arg position carries a fully-formed string. All `.mcp.json` mutations in the CLI flow through the shared `writeMcpEntry` helper to guarantee uniform key-preservation semantics and the abort-on-malformed-JSON contract.

**Per-file failures roll into the warning; only malformed `.mcp.json` aborts the whole run.** An unwritable agent file must not prevent the methodology stub, slash commands, MCP merge, and launcher from being installed. Each per-artifact helper returns a result object rather than throwing for routine failures; the orchestrator logs the failure line, appends the artifact to the skipped/failed list for the warning, and continues with the next artifact. The user gets a partial install plus a warning enumerating exactly what failed and how to finish it, rather than a full abort that left the project in a half-installed state with no log of how far it got. The only condition that aborts the whole run is malformed `.mcp.json` — because continuing past a parse failure would write the `aide` entry into the wrong place — plus unrecoverable process-level failures (out of disk, signal interrupt). Exit code 0 covers the install-completed-with-skips case; non-zero is reserved for actual failures.

**Per-file log is terse and color-free; loud ASCII is reserved for the warning block.** One line per artifact, status prefix first (`[created]` or `[exists]`; `[failed]` on a per-file write failure that rolls into the warning), host-relative path next, short reason last. No colors, no spinners, no progress bars, no ASCII art in the per-file lines. The flat log is what makes the warning's loud border register — visual rhythm is a budget, and the warning gets all of it.

**Framework targeting is Claude Code only at fixed paths; multi-framework probing is deferred.** The CLI writes to `.claude/commands/aide/` for slash commands, `.claude/agents/aide/` for pipeline agents, `.claude/skills/` for skill templates, `.mcp.json` for the `aide` MCP server entry, `CLAUDE.md` (or the existing host config the stub discovers) for the methodology pointer, `.aide/docs/` for the methodology doc set, `.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs` for the launcher, and `.aide/config/brain.aide` for the bundled brain template (first cold install only — the file is user-owned forever after). Multi-framework detection (Cursor, Windsurf, Zed, Copilot) is out of scope at cold-start because the CLI runs before any agent session exists and there is no user-bandwidth channel to confirm a detected framework choice — IDE selection is the one inherent decision the CLI surfaces, and it does so through an explicit `--ide <choice>` flag, never through a filesystem probe.

**The CLI remains a single async IIFE with a tiny, hand-parsed flag surface and zero interactive prompts.** The recognized flags are `--help`, `--ide <choice>`, and `--brain <integration>` — each parsed inline by a small reader function with no third-party dependency. `--ide` and `--brain` are power-user shortcuts to supply, up-front, a value that selects which methodology-layer artifact bundle scaffolds (`--ide` picks the IDE preview wiring; `--brain` picks the bundled `brain.aide` template). Neither flag prompts the user, neither flag accepts a backend-specific datum, and `--brain` defaults to `obsidian` when omitted so the no-flag invocation produces the same on-disk result as `--brain obsidian`. Zero prompts, zero `y/N` gates, zero in-run input requests; the `npx` invocation is itself the opt-in. Flags that gate behavior unrelated to artifact-bundle selection — verbosity toggles, dry-run, force-overwrite, framework selection, dotfile discovery, network opt-in, telemetry — are out of scope; the non-help flag surface is reserved exclusively for the two artifact-bundle selectors. The non-interactive shape is what makes the CLI safe to run unattended and what forces every backend-specific decision into the `/aide:brain config` agent-session surface where it belongs.

## Good examples

A developer runs the installer in a cold project with no flags. The methodology layer lands in full, the bundled `brain.aide` template lands at `.aide/config/brain.aide` with YAML null at the unwired slot, no `.mcp.json` brain entry is written, no entry-point artifact is seeded, and the warning routes the user to `/aide:brain config` and to a CLI re-run for IDE:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
[created] .aide/docs/index.md — methodology doc set index
[created] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — canonical AIDE spec
[created] .aide/docs/aide-template.md — canonical AIDE template
[created] .aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md — canonical progressive-disclosure doc
[created] .aide/docs/agent-readable-code.md — canonical agent-readable-code doc
[created] .aide/docs/automated-qa.md — canonical automated-qa doc
[created] .aide/docs/plan-aide.md — canonical plan.aide doc
[created] .aide/docs/todo-aide.md — canonical todo.aide doc
[created] .aide/docs/cascading-alignment.md — canonical cascading-alignment doc
[created] .claude/commands/aide.md — /aide orchestrator
[created] .claude/commands/aide/research.md — /aide:research
[created] .claude/commands/aide/spec.md — /aide:spec
[created] .claude/commands/aide/synthesize.md — /aide:synthesize
[created] .claude/commands/aide/plan.md — /aide:plan
[created] .claude/commands/aide/build.md — /aide:build
[created] .claude/commands/aide/qa.md — /aide:qa
[created] .claude/commands/aide/fix.md — /aide:fix
[created] .claude/commands/aide/upgrade.md — /aide:upgrade
[created] .claude/commands/aide/update-playbook.md — /aide:update-playbook
[created] .claude/commands/aide/refactor.md — /aide:refactor
[created] .claude/commands/aide/align.md — /aide:align
[created] .claude/commands/aide/brain.md — /aide:brain
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-spec-writer.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-domain-expert.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-strategist.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-implementor.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-qa.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-auditor.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-aligner.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-explorer.md — pipeline agent
[created] .claude/skills/study-playbook/SKILL.md — skill template
[created] .claude/skills/brain/SKILL.md — skill template
[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry
[created] .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs — aide-tree launcher
[created] .aide/config/brain.aide — bundled brain template (--brain obsidian default; args[3] is YAML null until /aide:brain config fills it)

============================================================
  SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED
============================================================

  The installer placed every methodology-layer artifact it
  could safely write and scaffolded the bundled brain.aide
  template with a structurally unwired slot. Filling the
  unwired slot, deriving the brain MCP entry, and seeding
  the entry-point artifacts require decisions made inside
  an agent session and were NOT done by this command.

  Deferred (each item names how to finish it):
    1. Brain wiring — open Claude Code and run /aide; on the first run, /aide:brain config will fill the unwired slot in .aide/config/brain.aide, derive the brain MCP entry through cli/sync, and seed the four entry-point artifacts into your brain.
    2. IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>

============================================================
```

Exit code 0. The post-install state on disk:

- `.aide/docs/`, `.claude/commands/aide/`, `.claude/agents/aide/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `.mcp.json` all exist with the methodology layer in place.
- `.aide/config/brain.aide` exists with the bundled template's bytes. Its `mcpServerConfig.args` carries YAML null at `args[3]` (a bare `-` line) — no literal-string placeholder, no `<BRAIN_PATH>`, no `__UNWIRED__`, just a structural null the parser will map to JS `null`. The directory's user-owned ownership clock is now running.
- `.mcp.json` contains exactly one server entry: `{ "mcpServers": { "aide": { /* ... */ } } }`. There is no `brain` key — that lands later via `cli/sync` invoked from `/aide:brain config` after the user has filled the null slot.
- The brain root directory is not created. No entry-point artifact is seeded.

The user opens Claude Code, runs `/aide`, and the orchestrator routes the first-run brain wiring through `/aide:brain config`. The integration's `config` body section asks for whatever its backend needs (an absolute brain path, an API token, a connection URI), edits the just-scaffolded `brain.aide` in place to fill the null slot(s), runs `cli/sync` to derive the `.mcp.json` `brain` entry, and seeds the four entry-point artifacts via the brain's own MCP write tools. From that point forward the project is fully wired.

A developer runs the installer with `--ide zed --brain obsidian` on a cold project. The methodology layer lands, IDE preview is wired up, the bundled template selected by `--brain obsidian` is scaffolded with `args[3]` as YAML null, the warning's IDE entry is omitted, and the brain entry still routes to `/aide:brain config`:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init --ide zed --brain obsidian
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
... (per-file log continues for every methodology-layer artifact)
[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry
[created] .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs — aide-tree launcher
[created] .aide/config/brain.aide — bundled brain template (--brain obsidian default; args[3] is YAML null until /aide:brain config fills it)
[created] .zed/settings.json — IDE preview wiring (zed)

============================================================
  SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED
============================================================

  Deferred (each item names how to finish it):
    1. Brain wiring — open Claude Code and run /aide; on the first run, /aide:brain config will fill the unwired slot in .aide/config/brain.aide and walk you through deriving the brain MCP entry and seeding the entry-point artifacts.

============================================================
```

The IDE category was resolved up-front by `--ide zed`. The `--brain obsidian` argument selected the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()` helper as the source of `brain.aide`'s bytes — passing `--brain obsidian` explicitly produces the same result as the no-flag default. Brain wiring is still deferred because the bytes the CLI scaffolded carry a structurally unwired slot (YAML null at `args[3]`), and filling that slot needs an agent session.

A developer runs the installer a second time on a project where the methodology layer was already installed and the user has since run `/aide:brain config` to fill the null slot, run sync, and seed the entry-point artifacts:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init --ide zed
[exists] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub already present
[exists] .aide/docs/index.md — already present
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
... (every methodology artifact reports [exists])
[exists] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry already present
[exists] .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs — aide-tree launcher already present
[exists] .aide/config/brain.aide — already present (user-owned; not touched)
[exists] .zed/settings.json — IDE preview wiring (zed) already present

Already set up.
```

No files were modified. The CLI checked `.aide/config/brain.aide`'s presence, reported `[exists]`, and did not read its bytes — even though the user has since hand-edited the file to fill `args[3]` with their absolute brain path, refined the body sections to suit their workflow, and renamed the brain. The CLI does not introspect any `.aide/config/` file once the directory's ownership clock has started. Every methodology artifact reported `[exists]`, the brain template reported `[exists]`, no inherent-decision category remains unresolved, so the warning block is suppressed and the plain completion line prints. Exit code 0.

A developer runs the installer against a project whose `.mcp.json` has a trailing comma from a hand-edit mistake:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
Error: .mcp.json exists but contains invalid JSON. Fix the syntax error and re-run.
```

Exit code 1. No files were written. Not the methodology stub, not the doc set, not the slash commands, not the agents, not the launcher — the CLI aborts the whole run because continuing would write the `aide` entry into a project whose MCP wiring is already broken. The user fixes the syntax and re-runs; the second run installs cleanly.

A developer runs the installer against a project that already has `github` and a custom `local-llm` MCP server in `.mcp.json`:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
... (per-file log)
[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry merged
... (per-file log continues)

============================================================
  SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED
============================================================
  ...
============================================================
```

The post-merge `.mcp.json` carries `github`, `local-llm`, *and* `aide` — every existing entry preserved byte-for-byte, with `aide` added beside them. There is no `brain` key (cold install does not write one). The merge was purely additive, exactly as the parent intent's `.mcp.json` semantics require.

## Bad examples

The CLI writes a `.mcp.json` brain entry alongside `brain.aide`:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
... (per-file log)
[created] .aide/config/brain.aide — bundled brain template (obsidian default)
[created] .mcp.json — aide and brain MCP server entries
```

The CLI applied both InitSteps returned by `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` — the brain.aide-scaffold step (correct) and the MCP-entry-plan step (forbidden). The result is a `.mcp.json` whose `mcpServers["brain"]` entry was derived from a `brain.aide` whose `mcpServerConfig.args` carries YAML null at the unwired indexes — so the brain entry's args carry the same null indexes, exactly the shape `cli/sync` refuses at its boundary by exiting non-zero and the same shape that previously masked the silent-failure-at-launch bug when literal-string sentinels propagated byte-for-byte through both files. The brain MCP entry is owned by `cli/sync` invoked from `/aide:brain config` AFTER the user has filled the null slot(s); the cold-start CLI applies only the brain.aide-scaffold InitStep and must never apply the MCP-entry-plan step.

The CLI accepts a brain-shaped flag whose value is a backend-specific datum:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init --brain-path D:/data/my-brain-folder
... (per-file log)
[created] .aide/config/brain.aide — Brain config with args[3] filled from --brain-path
[created] .mcp.json — aide and brain MCP server entries
```

The CLI grew a brain-shaped flag whose value is a per-host backend datum (an absolute filesystem path), accepted it at install time, and used it to write a brain.aide whose `args[3]` carries the supplied path instead of YAML null. Multiple failures stack: the CLI now knows about path-shaped backends and the meaning of `args[3]` (failing the storage-agnostic invariant and the no-introspection-of-`mcpServerConfig.args` invariant), it would land a `.mcp.json` `brain` entry derived from the now-fully-armed args (failing the no-cold-install-brain-MCP-entry contract), and the flag class itself is forbidden by the parent's "any brain-shaped flag whose value is a backend-specific datum" undesired outcome. The forbidden class is the whole shape — value-bearing brain flags — not a list of names: `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`, `--brain-token`, `--brain-url`, `--brain-name`, and any other flag whose value is a path, token, URI, or other backend-specific datum are all out. The single recognized brain-shaped flag is `--brain <integration>` whose effect is template-bundle selection only — the value names which bundled template helper produces the bytes (today `obsidian`), and no backend-specific datum changes hands. Passing `--brain obsidian` produces the same result as the no-flag default; passing `--brain-path <anything>` is a regression.

The CLI prompts the user for a brain path interactively:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
Where is your brain located? Enter an absolute path: _
```

The CLI surfaced an `--interactive` mode (or read stdin without a flag, or popped an `AskUserQuestion`-shaped terminal prompt) and asked the user for a backend-specific value at install time. The cold-start CLI runs in a plain terminal without an agent on the other end of the wire, has no UI to surface a structured question, and must complete its happy path with zero interaction beyond running `npx` itself. Backend-specific values belong to the integration's prose surfaced through `/aide:brain config` inside an agent session that can hold a conversation, not to a one-shot terminal invocation that cannot. Every prompt-shaped surface — stdin reads, `--interactive` flags, env-var reads with confirmation — is forbidden.

The CLI creates the brain root directory on cold install:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
... (per-file log)
[created] D:/Users/dev/Documents/aide-brain/ — created brain root directory
[created] .aide/config/brain.aide — Brain config pointing at brain root
```

The CLI reached outside the host project root, picked a default location based on the OS, and created an empty directory there as the future brain root. This combines two failures: it picks a backend-specific value (an absolute filesystem path) without the bandwidth to confirm it with the user, and it touches filesystem state outside the host project on a tool the user invoked from inside the host project. The cold-start CLI's filesystem write surface is bounded to the host project root; brain root creation is the integration's responsibility and happens later through `/aide:brain config` after the user has chosen and configured a backend.

The CLI guesses a default brain path on a no-flag run and fills it into the scaffolded `brain.aide`:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
... (per-file log)
[created] .aide/config/brain.aide — Brain config (auto-detected sibling brain folder filled into args[3])
[created] .mcp.json — aide and brain MCP server entries
Detected sibling brain folder — wired brain MCP server
```

The folder the CLI discovered is actually the user's spouse's personal journal directory, not an AIDE brain. The CLI found it from a filesystem scan, replaced the bundled template's YAML null at `args[3]` with the discovered path, and silently committed the user's pipeline to writing research artifacts into a directory they never consented to. There is no correct fallback here: filesystem scans, env-var reads, platform defaults, sibling-folder probes — every form of guessing-without-asking is wrong. The cold-start CLI scaffolds the bundled template byte-for-byte with YAML null at the unwired slot(s) and does not fill those slots from any source; the user's brain location is asked-for and confirmed inside the agent session via `/aide:brain config` and written into the file there.

The CLI silently replaces the user's existing `.mcp.json`:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry
```

The user had `github`, a legacy `obsidian` key from a pre-rework install, and two custom MCP servers configured. All of them are gone, overwritten by a fresh file containing only the `aide` entry. The CLI decided the on-disk config was stale and assumed it could replace it — the exact failure mode the parent intent's "never overwrites" contract exists to prevent. The merge must be additive: every existing entry preserved byte-for-byte, with `aide` added beside them. The legacy `obsidian` key in particular must survive untouched here as user data the package never reads, writes, removes, or detects; no migration of any kind happens through the cold-start CLI, and no migration happens through `cli/sync` either (the package retired legacy-key migration entirely; pre-rework hosts carry their `obsidian` key as user data forever, exactly as the parent intent specifies).

The CLI surfaces skips inline with the per-file log instead of as a loud block:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
[created] .aide/docs/aide-template.md — canonical AIDE template
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
WARNING: skipped because file exists
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md — pipeline agent
[exists] .claude/agents/aide/aide-spec-writer.md — already present
WARNING: skipped because file exists
...
Done. Open Claude Code and run /aide:upgrade to reconcile skipped files.
```

The warnings are structurally inline with the `[created]`/`[exists]` lines, using the same font weight, the same indentation, and the same width as the rest of the log. A user skimming the output reads every line as routine status and never registers that the install is incomplete. The warning has to break the log's visual rhythm with borders and uppercase heading to register, and it has to appear once at the end with all skipped files and deferred categories aggregated, not interleaved with routine status lines.

The CLI routes the warning's brain item to `/aide` instead of `/aide:brain config`:

```
============================================================
  SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED
============================================================

  Deferred (each item names how to finish it):
    1. Brain wiring — open Claude Code and run /aide; the orchestrator will detect that your brain is not wired and walk you through configuration.
    2. IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>

============================================================
```

The warning names `/aide` itself as the brain follow-up surface, implying the orchestrator owns brain-wiring inline recovery. The orchestrator does not own that responsibility — `/aide:brain config` does. Naming `/aide` for brain wiring leaves the user running the orchestrator and waiting for an interview that never comes (because the orchestrator forwards brain wiring to `/aide:brain config` itself). Every CLI-emitted reference to a brain follow-up must name `/aide:brain config` directly so the user lands on the surface that has the wiring flow. The same rule applies to any other removed slash command — the parent intent's "directs the user to a removed slash command" undesired outcome rules out naming any non-shipped surface, with brain-wiring routing being the most common failure mode.

The CLI aborts the entire run on a single file's write failure:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
[created] .aide/docs/index.md — methodology doc set index
Error: EACCES: permission denied, open '.aide/docs/aide-spec.md'
```

Exit code 1. The methodology stub made it in, nothing else did — not the rest of the doc set, not the slash commands, not the agents, not the MCP entry, not the launcher. A single per-file failure must roll into the warning alongside skipped files and let the rest of the install continue; only malformed `.mcp.json` and unrecoverable process-level failures abort the whole run.

The CLI prints the warning block over an all-clean re-run:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
[exists] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub already present
... (every artifact reports [exists])
[exists] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry already present
[exists] .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs — aide-tree launcher already present

============================================================
  SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED
============================================================

  (no skipped files, no deferred categories)

============================================================
```

Every artifact is `[exists]` (including `.aide/config/brain.aide`, which the user has long since wired through `/aide:brain config`), IDE was resolved in a prior run — there is genuinely nothing to warn about. The CLI prints an empty warning box anyway. The warning suppression rule is exact: when the skip/failed list is empty AND no inherent-decision category is unresolved, print `Already set up.` (or `Install complete.`). Printing an empty loud box trains users to ignore the warning surface, which destroys its signal in the runs that genuinely need it. (The brain-wiring follow-up is owned by `/aide:brain config` regardless of CLI state — the warning does not need a brain entry on a fully clean re-run because the CLI cannot tell from disk state whether the user has finished wiring. On any run where another category is incomplete the warning carries the brain follow-up unconditionally; on an otherwise-clean run the warning is suppressed entirely so it does not lose signal.)

The CLI prompts the user mid-run for any reason:

```
$ npx aidemd-mcp init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
The .aide/docs/ folder will contain the canonical methodology files. Continue? [y/N]
```

The happy path must be zero-interaction. The `npx` invocation is the opt-in; a confirmation prompt adds friction to a command whose whole purpose is to be runnable unattended. The interactive surface for any decision the cold-start CLI cannot make alone is the agent session reachable through the warning's follow-up surfaces, not the terminal.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` -- root project intent; source of the cli/init contract wording the body cascades from — the `--brain <integration>` template-bundle-selection rule defaulting to `obsidian`, the methodology-layer-plus-bundled-brain-template-with-YAML-null-at-unwired-slots cold-install partition, the never-write-`.mcp.json`-brain-entry / never-seed-entry-point-artifacts / never-create-brain-root / never-ask-for-backend-specific-value enumeration, the directory-as-ownership-boundary guarantee for `.aide/config/`, the no-introspection-of-`mcpServerConfig.args` invariant, the YAML-null-not-literal-sentinel design, the legacy-`obsidian`-key-migration-dropped-entirely position, and the per-category follow-up surface routing for the terminal warning.
- `.aide/handoff.aide` -- mid-rework anti-regression invariants; source of the architectural intent items #2 (default scaffolded launcher and `args[3]` semantics for the bundled obsidian template), #11 (`provisionBrain` takes no `brainPath` parameter at any layer), #12 (cli/init contract: accepts only `--brain <integration>`, applies brain.aide-scaffold InitStep only), #20 (`mcpServerConfig.args` typed `(string | null)[]`, YAML null is the structural unwired-slot signal, no literal-string sentinels), #21 (provisionBrain's two-step pipeline; entry-point artifacts seeded by integration prose at `/aide:brain config`, not provisionBrain), #23 (`cli/sync` refuses null-bearing args at the boundary), and #26 (legacy `mcpServers.obsidian` migration dropped entirely). Anchors the Strategy paragraphs on why the CLI applies only the brain.aide-scaffold InitStep and on the storage-agnostic flag boundary, and the Bad examples on the MCP-entry-plan-step regression and the legacy-obsidian-key-survives-untouched framing.
- `src/.aide` -- parent spec; source of the two-delivery-channels frame (handshake teaching surface plus install action), the two-entry-points-into-a-shared-install-service shape that scopes what the CLI is responsible for at the cold-start hop, and the per-consumer-ownership-of-`brain.aide` invariant that justifies the CLI never reading the bytes it just scaffolded.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` -- sibling spec for the install-service module the CLI delegates to; pinned the two-InitStep contract the cold-start CLI applies one of (the brain.aide-scaffold step), the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()` no-parameter helper shape, and the explicit MCP-entry-plan step that the CLI deliberately does not apply. Anchors the Strategy paragraph on calling `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` and applying only the first InitStep.
- `src/cli/sync/.aide` -- sibling spec for the brain MCP entry writer; pinned the null-bearing-args refusal at the boundary that justifies why the CLI must not write the brain entry on cold install (the entry derived from a null-armed brain.aide would be exactly what sync refuses). Anchors the Strategy paragraph on MCP merge restricting itself to the `aide` key and the Bad example on writing the brain entry alongside brain.aide.
- `.aide/docs/aide-template.md` -- canonical body-section contract for the spec itself: Context explains why the module exists, Strategy distills decisions, Good/Bad examples are real output, References logs what was actually used during synthesis.
